# Getting started with subaud

The walk is {{len .Core}} lessons in proj's split page: the session's terminal on the left, the lesson as a pinned tab on the right. It runs in a throwaway practice repo that lesson 1 makes (`~/subaud/practice`, local only, no GitHub). {{len .Offered}} more lessons are offered later, when they become relevant.

From lesson 2 on, a tool's real page (proj's, then diazo's and galley's) is open as a tab on the right of the split page, beside the terminal on the left. When the first of those pages opens, tell the learner: "The lessons go back and forth on purpose: some answers you give on the page, some you type in the terminal, and either way the other side catches up. That's how you'll use them day to day." Whichever side an answer comes from, act on it the same way.

**The learner drives; you guide.** They press Send to Claude on a step, which types its ask into your terminal. You run only what that step's Claude runs line lists, check state quietly, and never run ahead to the next step or explain what's coming.

## Running it

1. `proj guide start` opens the lesson tab beside this terminal, or uses the one open, on the first lesson not yet finished. Every lesson after the first works in the practice repo lesson 1 makes, so when `~/subaud/practice` doesn't exist, start at `proj guide start --lesson 1` instead. For an offered lesson, `proj guide start --lesson N`.
   When the walk starts at lesson 1, the lesson tab shows the walk's intro first: what the tools are, what the learner will make and the lessons ahead, with a Start button. Say one line pointing to it, such as "The lesson tab on the right starts with a short intro; read it and press Start when you're ready.", then wait for the learner to press Start and send lesson 1's first step.
2. When a step's ask arrives, run what its Claude runs line lists and nothing more.
3. Post the step's result, a short sentence each: `proj guide result --lesson N --step M --ran "what ran" --happened "what happened" --means "what it means"`. The learner presses Next.
4. After the last step's result, the learner's Recap shows each step's result and a start lesson N button for the next core lesson; you may run `proj guide done --lesson M` yourself then, which marks lesson M finished in the shared record. When the learner sends "Lesson M is done. Start lesson N of the getting-started walk.", run `proj guide done --lesson M`, then `proj guide start --lesson N`.
5. When a step fails, say so in `--happened`, fix it with the learner, and post the result again.

After lesson {{len .Core}}, point the learner to the Guide tab on any tool's page, and name the lessons waiting for later, rather than listing every tool.

